    "issue": "picoduck/wollipog#1397",
    "capturedOn": "2026-09-18",
    "question": "In `default` and `auto` permission modes, with the stdio control channel the runner uses, does a SUBAGENT's Bash call produce a `can_use_tool` control_request, does that frame carry `parent_tool_use_id`, and what placement fields does it carry?",
    "method": "A throwaway project under /tmp, driven by headless `claude` launched with the exact interactive arg set apps/runner/src/drivers/claude-code.ts builds. A harness read the stdout stream, answered every can_use_tool frame the way ClaudeCodeDriver.resolvePermission does, and joined each frame against the Bash tool_use the model actually issued (whose own `parent_tool_use_id` names the spawning Task call). No hook was installed, so nothing could suppress or fabricate a frame. The probe harness is not committed.",
    "claudeVersions": {
      "2.1.277": "the version `claude` on PATH resolves to, i.e. the one the runner launches",
      "2.1.270": "the version docs/DRIVERS.md records its other measurements against; also measured, same result, frames omitted here because 2.1.277 is what the runner launches"
    },
    "finding": "A subagent's Bash call DOES produce a can_use_tool frame in both modes. The frame never carries `parent_tool_use_id` — the message envelope is exactly {type, request_id, request} — and carries no `cwd`. A subagent is distinguished by `request.agent_id`, the same opaque id the PreToolUse hook payload carries (#1361). The only placement field is `request.blocked_path`, present only when the escalation reason is a specific path; it is absolute and resolved from the issuer's real shell directory.",
    "sanitization": "__REPO__ is the throwaway project directory; __WORKTREE__ is a disposable directory inside it that stands in for a runner-managed worktree. No tokens, credentials, or real home paths were present in or removed from these frames."
  },
